the dirtbag ground score is an ultragrid UL rolltop bag for your bike that can convert to a tote or crossbody bag. it has a voile strap closure system that can attach to a basket or a rack. it's made by one guy, ben teames, in portland oregon, and is about as simple as it gets when it comes to features, which i like. it comes in a few different fun colors.

dillon sent this bag to me because we had been talking about making something in this style, simple enough to stuff groceries into but with a perfect fit for a small wald basket. his idea was to do a waxed canvas, as a little extra weight certainly wouldn't hurt. when he sent me the link to this product though, it seemed like all the boxes had been checked, and someone had already made our hypothetical bag. 

i've been using it since april, on two different bikes, with a few different attachment setups, and all have worked well. i had it on my GT with the small wald basket, and on the surly with the nano straps attached to just the rack, as well as on a large wald basket. 

the top of the bag rolls down to secure the load, and the tape on the opening of the bag has a buckle to keep everything pinned to the attachment points. the setup is very simple, but effective. 

some things i've noticed are that the position on which you set up the voile anchors determine how large or small your load can be. for example, if you attach them to just a rack, your load cannot be taller in the bag than about 7 or 8 inches, because you won't have enough material to fold over for the buckle to meet it. this can be tricky for those grocery trips when you need to get oil, cans of beans, honey, lettuce, and other bulky, heavy items. similarly, if the voile bits are attached too high, like on the wall of a basket for example, your load is less secure because there's no tension to pin it down when you have something small in the bag, like a couple books or a small item. there is a little bit of finagling needed to get it all properly secured sometimes, but i think it's worth it.

when not in use the bag rolls up really small and i keep it in my stem bag for convenience. i've actually taken off the included strappy bits because i simply don't use them, opting instead for just the bag functionality. 

i've used the bag on many grocery trips, runs from the ceramics studio, hella commutes, trips up the nearby mountains, in the rain, and i've gotten minimal wear on it. the only thing is that there's been a tiny bit of abrasion on the base, which when i noticed i immediately stuck a gear patch on. even ultragrid, while awesome, has its limits! 

having used it since april, i feel really satisfied! my freak tendencies force me into purchasing mostly japanese cottage bike and hike products, but it's great to support some domestic makers too. the only thing i would change is getting it in pink instead of coyote because the pink is toooooo cute.

